<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vistula</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Wisła</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vistula is the longest river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, about 1,050 kilometres long, rising in the Silesian Beskids and flowing past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> to the Baltic near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a>. Its basin has been the core of the Polish lands since the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and was earlier home to Germanic and Baltic peoples. Grain shipped down the river to Gdańsk was the basis of Polish trade from the 15th to the 17th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vistula.jpg" alt="Vistula" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mariusz Guć · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dolina-dolnej-wis%C5%82y.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
